1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:20,304
Use the school sequence aloud: technical quality, rate, regularity, P
waves, PR and QRS measurements, then ST and T review with QT when
indicated. A familiar-looking rhythm is not permission to skip the
later checks, particularly medication risk or changing ST findings.

2
00:00:20,304 --> 00:00:40,344
The order structures reporting; it does not replace rapid patient
assessment. Check responsiveness, breathing, circulation and
instability before a leisurely strip analysis. If there is no
effective circulation, follow the applicable resuscitation pathway
instead of completing a classroom worksheet first.

3
00:00:40,344 --> 00:00:59,664
Confirm that this is the right patient and a current tracing. Check
speed, gain, calibration and whether intended leads are available.
Look for baseline movement, signal dropout or interference. These
checks prevent a technical distortion being promoted into a clinical
diagnosis.

4
00:00:59,664 --> 00:01:17,352
Repeat an uninterpretable tracing after correcting the cause when
feasible. Record any persistent limitation. Do not delay assessment or
time-critical treatment while trying to produce a perfect printout,
and do not claim a reliable interpretation of obscured waveforms.

5
00:01:17,352 --> 00:01:34,200
Calculate the ventricular rate using the method that fits the observed
regularity. Then describe whether the pattern is regular, repeats an
irregular grouping, or has no repeating RR pattern. Use a longer
sample or calipers when the distinction is unclear.

6
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:57,264
Count atrial activity separately when P or flutter waves outnumber QRS
complexes. A ventricular rate alone cannot reveal the atrial-to-
ventricular conduction ratio. Conversely, an irregularly irregular RR
pattern is not enough to diagnose atrial fibrillation unless atrial
activity and technical quality support it.

7
00:01:57,264 --> 00:02:15,384
Inspect more than one beat and, when useful, another lead. Ask whether
P waves are visible, whether their shape is consistent, and whether
there is a reliable relationship to each QRS. P waves may be buried in
T waves or rapid complexes rather than truly absent.

8
00:02:15,384 --> 00:02:36,432
Look in both directions: is every QRS preceded by an appropriate P,
and is every P followed by a QRS? The second question detects failed
conduction that a glance at conducted beats misses. Independent P and
QRS schedules raise a different question from merely changing P-wave
shape.

9
00:02:36,432 --> 00:02:55,608
Measure PR in conducted beats and compare successive intervals.
Measure QRS width rather than describing it by visual impression
alone. At the recovery step, examine ST displacement and T-wave
changes; a normal-looking rhythm name does not complete this part of
the assessment.

10
00:02:55,608 --> 00:03:17,952
Record unclear boundaries and acquisition limitations. QTc deserves
attention in syncope, medication exposure or a suspicious recovery
pattern. Detailed diagnostic twelve-lead patterns belong to the later
school course; recognizing a concerning finding here should trigger
appropriate communication, not an invented diagnosis.

11
00:03:17,952 --> 00:03:37,608
On this original example, verified settings and a clean strip show a
regular ventricular rate of ninety, consistent P waves before each
QRS, PR zero point one eight seconds and QRS zero point one zero
seconds. Describe those observations before applying the sinus-rhythm
label.

12
00:03:37,608 --> 00:03:58,032
Complete the report with what was not established: absence of obvious
ST change in one monitoring lead does not exclude ACS. Add symptoms,
pulse, perfusion, whether a diagnostic recording was obtained, and the
reassessment plan. This is a reasoned interpretation, not just a
rhythm name.
